        "id": "RA_FRESH_2026_07_10_CHAIN_EXTRACT_BIND_v6",
        "title": "Chain extract bind v6",
        "layout": "##########\n##PLBS####\n##########\n##.....###\n#.CCG...##\n#@###MG###\n##########",
        "lineage": {
          "source": "fresh_design_review_loop",
          "created_by": "codex",
          "created_at": "2026-07-10"
        },
        "design_notes": "Fresh Reality Anchor candidate from the 2026-07-10 design-review loop. The compact claim is a fixed P/L chain extract into fixed B/S material bind: the shortest route uses two consecutive P-side chain pushes, leaves a residual crate covering the left target, converts the chain-end crate into sticky material, merges it with the waiting M, then uses an L-side pull to move the merged sticky vertical body so the lower waiting sticky cell covers the lower target. Full graph analysis is complete: shortest cost 9, 68 reachable states, 130 legal transitions, and 1 winning state. Event-count probes prove every win requires push_object >= 2, force_chain >= 1, pull_object >= 1, box_to_sticky >= 1, sticky_merge >= 1, and move_sticky_rigid >= 1; force_chain >= 2 is only an intended-shortest-route fact because a 21-step alternate uses one force_chain. Fixed-anchor scans report no reachable P/L or B/S anchor shift. Goal-prune checks retain both targets: removing the left target admits a one-push shortest win, while removing the lower target collapses the puzzle to 2 steps and bypasses pull, material bind, and sticky-rigid movement. Opening comfort and redundant-element prune were completed for this exact v6. Independent evidence review_2 returned supports_with_caveats / required_action:none; independent puzzle critic review_2 returned proposal_ready_with_caveats / required_action:none, supporting aesthetic 4 floor and difficulty 3 floor, but not 5/4.\n",
